WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:03.778
Control voltage connects modular synthesizers.

2
00:00:03.778 --> 00:00:09.468
Through a DC-coupled audio interface, each input channel becomes a modulation source.

3
00:00:09.922 --> 00:00:14.314
Pick the interface, choose how many channels, and start the CV reader.

4
00:00:14.314 --> 00:00:17.452
A test signal stands in for a real interface here.

5
00:00:17.906 --> 00:00:20.285
Each channel shows its live voltage.

6
00:00:20.285 --> 00:00:27.356
Set its range, plus or minus five volts for Eurorack, its polarity and smoothing, and Calibrate to zero it.

7
00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:28.825
No interface?

8
00:00:28.825 --> 00:00:34.857
A Raspberry Pi with an analog to digital converter can send CV as OSC instead.

9
00:00:34.857 --> 00:00:38.337
Add an OSC CV channel and give it an address.

10
00:00:38.788 --> 00:00:41.812
Gamepads and HID controllers appear here, too.

11
00:00:41.812 --> 00:00:44.377
Every axis and button becomes a source.

12
00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:50.890
MCP lets an AI assistant, like Claude, drive the synth through the bridge in the tools folder.

13
00:00:50.890 --> 00:00:52.306
Connect to the bridge.

14
00:00:52.759 --> 00:00:54.823
Now the assistant is in control.

15
00:00:54.823 --> 00:01:00.759
It deepens the raster, widens it, turns it, and then builds a modulation patch from scratch.

16
00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:07.233
Anything an assistant can set, it can also read back: parameters, patches, even a screenshot.

17
00:01:07.233 --> 00:01:08.399
Next: the outputs.
